MY WHERE WINDS MEET REVIEW

+ you can choose a single mode for this game if you are not into a typical MMO;
+ Nice graphics;
+ Good customization;
+ the music is amazing;
+ The artstyle is very pretty;
+ lots of outfits you can get for free;
+ Wuxia styled combat with a lot of ways to improve your stats;
+ amazing exploration;
+ very good photo mode.

- Gen AI NPCs. I don't like Gen AI implemented in games in any way possible. Writers can always make a far better dialogue than a dialogue generated by a machine and activated by prompts. Also the Gen AI disclosure has appeared only some time on the steam page. This should be put right away so the customers should make an immediate decision rather than play and discover it later suddenly on their own.
- Voice over in English is very poorly done. I'd suggest switching to CN voice over immediately;
- Some players speculate that some translation is also AI generated.
- Being able to upload your voice and your real photo to a game feels very unsafe;
- Paid gacha and some extremely overpriced items (that ship prop). Typical to NetEase games, sadly. But personally I can't support it.
- combat feels a bit stiff. 
- when you run and stop there is no animation in-between. Character suddenly having a vertical position feel unnatural.

I would give the game 6/10

COMBAT AND MOVEMENT
the perfect dodge is inconsistent and the parry is weird, sometimes the timing feel too generous and sometimes it just doesnt work;

if you get hit once you will take the full damage of the whole combo because it stunlocks you so the boss fights are a series of parry checks that you can only miss once or twice (you need to perfect parry tens of times in sequence without missing to kill a boss), also a lot of bosses have hit kill abilities so that a minus;

the game gives you too much freedom in your movement which makes the game movement and collision is janky as hell, also the enemy physical abilities that track you is a pain in the ass to predict because they are inconsistent, sometimes they legit teleport 180 degrees to hit you from a dodge.

the parry and dodge button insta overwrites your attack order, and again that may be the reason that you character sometimes dont obey your inputs.

they should really make all mystical abilities useful (atleast in the PvE), cause for the bosses you are using like the same three abilities that actually works, other than that you are doing the basic combo;

boss fights are pretty straightforward you die until you learn the boss moves, then you basic combo and parry until they die, sometimes you use a ability that flashes on top of the boss;

the stealth play is actually ok, something i didnt expect;

the whole lightness is such a good concept, i wish they explored it more in combat;

the bosses and elites are hp sponges for the purpose of dragging out the fight for more opportunities of you messing up your parries and having to restart the fight, which can make the combat boring and frustrating;

i absolutely hate that the enemies dont have collision with eachother, also in group fights all the responsiveness bs and movement bugs get turned to max;

fighting the normal enemies you just dont feel the weigh of your weapons hitting them it feels like hitting them with pieces of cardboard;

another thing is the camera, you WILL get fked by the horrible camera a lot of times, and not just in tight spaces. also the umbrella take half of the screen so it's a completely unusable weapon;

i have to say that the two puppeteer fights were pretty good, one was a good introductory boss and the other had a cool gimmick, the other two that stands out is the monk boss and the wolfgirl, both cause they are incredibly hard to perfectly parry, not that it's a good thing, still when you get the hang of the fight and just parry everything i almost makes it worth;

something else is that the enemies levels with you, so you never feel like getting stronger, also most of the time when you defeat the bosses it doesn't give you anything interesting so you dont feel like progressing/evolving.

playing in the legend difficulty and i feel like the enemies just have too much hp making the combat feel too repetitive.

MENUS AND SYSTEMS
the AI chat bot is interesting but incredibly annoying;

the build system is okay except the blueprint, that does not work and your building will just float where you place the blueprint;

there is too many places you need to go to collect rewards in the menus, combined with the fact that the menus are a nightmare to navigate and there is too many events going on at once it just disincentivize you from caring about them;

menus so bad i dont even want to go change/upgrade my gear legit makes me laugh;

the whole illnesses system is poorly explained and the false accusations are such bs that makes you wonder why they put it in, also right now there is little to nothing online about this game so information is scarce.

GRAPHICS
the game is incredibly beautiful, you can take some really good screenshots in almost any place you stop;

the character creation is something i didnt go too deep into, but something i noticed is that all men are feminine except when old, and all the woman in the entire game looks the same (npcs and pcs), even some npcs that are supposed to be old mothers/grandmas look like they are 17yo;

the weapons all look the same inside their categories, so if you use dual blades you will look at the same looking blades for the whole game (potentially hundreds of hours) and if you want a different weapon you will need to spend money in the gacha for a chance of maybe getting what you want;

some of the clothes and accessories in the game are cool, and for some reason you can buy hairstyles? and they change your hair color? and dont change the color of your beard/brow? why?? most of the clothes and mounts you can buy are too nonsensical and ugly.

STORY
although linear the main and side stories are pretty good, some of the side quests i did was good, like the ugly lady, lone drinking man and the Lunar Goddess. And the main story actually got to me, at the end of the first chapter i had a feeling of emptyness, something i just experienced with nier automata.

I cant stress enough how much of a movie the story is, everything is predetermined, you can simply do a boss fight perfectly without taking a single instance of damage and the cutscene just shows you getting bodied;

ONLINE
let me start by saying that the PvP is a complete d0gsh!t; the ping, the movement, the camera, the overall combat, everything i said in the PvE combat aplies here as well except the movement is 10x worse somehow. There is no counterplay, there is no dodge, there is no parry, the hitboxes are completely off you get hit once and just get stunlocked to death so whoever manages to get the random stagger first wins the fight 100% of the time

The Boss fights are a laggy mess of button mashing and dps checks that takes 30 min because of how much health they have.


nothing groundbreaking but for free its a really good game definetly worthy your time if you play solo, im in solo lvl 71 and i just got to the end of chapter 1, didnt even get to the second region in the game so there is a lot of content;

i might have draw almost 100 times from the free tries you can get and i got nothing so dont be a loser, dont spend money on gacha games

playing solo is great, coop is good and online is literal c@ncer, the online play single handedly ruined it for me and turned my review negative

I love this game, after also playing Black Wukong.

However, I have to say Black Wukong is too difficult and lacks a lot of fun. I got stuck on a certain place and didn't play for a long time. I'm still on Chapter 4, cause it's too hard and too stressful for me.

But this game is different! This game gives you endless possibilities:

If you like a challenge, you can grind difficult dungeons or duel with other players;

If you prefer a more casual experience, you can complete the main and side quests;

If you like culture, you can play chess, mahjong, and fishing every day :);

If you like social, you can go to the multiplayer world;

If you're a lone wolf, you can play single-player.

The graphics are stunning; I can't help but take pictures in every spot. It presents a realistic traditional Chinese martial arts world, allowing us to understand an ancient country thousands of years ago, their lives, clothing, hobbies, etc. It's like watching a Chinese martial arts drama. But I can be the hero in this Jianghu world!!! 

For the downside, it might be that it has too much content, especially skills and equipment upgrades—it's overwhelming :S ... But you can take your time; you'll figure it out eventually.

The probability of gacha is way too low, but outfits are the least important thing in this game and have absolutely no impact on your actual combat power.

Therefore, I highly recommend it.

I am not the best reviewer in the world, but I will try my best, expecially since I saw most of the top reviews were made by players with less than 200 hours on the game.

To add further to the intro I will add that I play on Legendary for PvE difficulty and almost reached Mythic (maximum rank) in PvP.
Anyway, let's dive in into the first part of the review:

Pros:
- Stunning graphic;
- Beautiful landscapes and scenarios;
- amazing gameplay, seamless and satisfying;
- Many difficulties to choose to appease every player;
- Many bosses are hard, very hard, of course it depends largely on the difficulty chosen but if you are looking for a challenge, you will not be unsatisfied;
- So much to do and so many aspects in the game to delve into;
- PvE is well curated, if a player is skilled enough can understand every mechanic instantly as the game tells you what is happening and how to deal with it in every phase, no trial and error if you pay a bit of attention;
- PvP is great, non perfect but great, the basic of PvP is all about engages and trades, the learning curve is a bit steep but once you understand your combos and the enemy ones, it will be a real challenge of skill and reflexes. "If you know the enemy and know yourself, you need not fear the result of a hundred battles";
- Exploration is a true pleasure, the world is big and fully interactive, the main city is huge and there are interesting things on every corner, even if you are a try-hard do not fear to just run through the world and appreciate it, that's also a core component of this game and you do not want to miss that;
- Leisure activities are plenty and well done, the right amount of fun and easy to play / hard to master that makes it enjoyable on every level;
- You can talk with hundreds of NPCs, everyone of them is backed by an AI persona, a lot of stories to delve into and problems to solve, it just adds even more depth into the world than what already there is;
- The Story is incredible, I am one of the player that usually skips everything, but in this game both the campaign and side stories are really well done and skipping them would results in a big loss, even if you care only about speedrunning every content just follow them, trust me, also because many stories bring very deep and unexpected tragic moments;
- It is fully Free to play, only the Battle Pass can bring some utilities (not necessary at all) but all the other purchases with money are all appearances;
- There are tons of outstanding appearances obtainable without spending a dime, mainly through challenges, achivements or purchasable with in-game currency;
- No strict daily activities, 99% of the game content is based on weekly cap, so you don't feel it as a second job where you have to do always the same boring things every day or you're gonna lose progress;
- You can see what you got from every region, and for almost everything there are clues, that means you do not need third party interactive maps or other boring assets but you can actually use a bit of your time plus a bit of brain to complete and do everything.

Cons:
- The UI is confusing to say the least and there are way too many menus and currencies;
- The game doesn't explain a lot of stuff, that was a pro for me as I love to explore a new world with its systems by myself but I understand that it can be a big con for many;
- It is still unclear the game development path, if we are going to quickly catch with the chinese version or not and when specific contents will be added.


Final consideration:

The game is a 10/10 masterpiece and my most sincere suggestion is to just delve into it without any guide/build/map or whatever, there is no need for them and by cutting those "helps" off you will truly get a once in a life experience.

See you in the Jianghu wanderer.

Once you immerse into the jianghu, it's hard to step back. Very impressive open-world experience, tons of activities to do, good world-building and a well-illustrated Wuxia story.

Some flaws that you may consider before playing: 
- English translation and AI voice over is very cringe; 
- The performance may drop once you unlock Kaifeng; 
- The UI can be very confusing at first with 100 menus, currencies and shops; 
- Combat is not that hard as other souls game as it's more towards to casual and co-op players; it's still challenging but may not meet the expectation if you're looking for a hardcore soul game. There are still some bosses which are very hard, though.

Anyway it's free. So why don't you give it a try?

pretty fun, you just gotta get used to the dodge mechanic like in elder ring or dark souls, but the game is pretty helpful as you can be assisted with that if you are not good/used to it;
other than that it looks like this is an f2p game, the only thing i think you can buy is clothes and i dont think they do anything other than appearance
this feels like a mix between souls-like games, gacha games and coop/minigame games

You know, this is one of those games with ridiculously ambitious plans. The difference is: unlike most of them, the execution actually delivers.
I usually don’t like soulslikes. To me they’re too much pattern memorization disguised as “difficulty”. I also played a lot of Lost Ark back in the day (like, a lot).
And yet this game feels great, probably because the boss grind is balanced by genuinely enjoyable exploration and an immersive world you actually want to believe in.

I’m fairly sure I’m playing it “wrong” (probably). I’m 34 hours in and I still haven’t unlocked the second region, because I can’t beat “Mastermind” (the witch) on Legendary difficulty. Yes, I came here to suffer, please don’t interrupt. I’m also stuck on the blacksmith guy questline. On the bright side, I explored the first region to about 7.5k exploration points (don’t remember the exact percentage).

Plenty of reasons to try this game:
- a large, immersive world packed with secrets and things to discover;
- it’s free-to-play. There’s a resource gate, but loosening it costs about ~$20, and anything beyond that is cosmetic (at least for now);
- it looks great and seems well optimized: minimal area streaming hiccups, and loading screens don’t take forever;
- you can play casually on any difficulty below Legendary (there’s even a deflect assist). Or you can choose pain;
- nice music. Not mind-blowing, but fitting and never annoying;
- 11 xianxia/10.

What I personally didn’t like:
- maybe I’m missing something (the game doesn’t explain everything at first glance), but it feels like some IMPORTANT mechanics are tied to online play. Recently my character broke their legs from a fall, and apparently Bone Crack can’t be treated by NPCs. I had to summon an actual “Chinese” doctor (another player). Why? This isn’t an MMO, and if I’m playing solo, I’d really like the experience to stay solo. 
UPD: I also joined a healer sect, and the only way to rank up is by healing other players in co-op. Why, though? The game already has clans, which are the actual social/co-op mechanic. This sect isn’t a clan, yet it still locks progression behind online play in a game that isn’t an MMO.

